(; doc: The homogeneous version of `HAdd`: `a + b : α` where `a b : α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Add ;)
Add : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An `AddGroup` is an `AddMonoid` with a unary `-` satisfying `-a + a = 0`.  There is also a binary operation `-` such that `a - b = a + -b`, with a default so that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.  Use `AddGroup.ofLeftAxioms` or `AddGroup.ofRightAxioms` to define an additive group structure on a type with the minimum proof obligations.  [Wikidata Q83478](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q83478) ;)
(; //LEAN:AddGroup ;)
AddGroup : (A : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An `AddMonoid` is an `AddSemigroup` with an element `0` such that `0 + a = a + 0 = a`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddMonoid ;)
AddMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Bundling an `Add` and `Zero` structure together without any axioms about their compatibility. See `AddZeroClass` for the additional assumption that 0 is an identity. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddZero ;)
AddZero : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Typeclass for expressing that a type `M` with addition and a zero satisfies `0 + a = a` and `a + 0 = a` for all `a : M`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddZeroClass ;)
AddZeroClass : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A library note is identified by the name of its declaration, and its content should be contained in its doc-string. ;)
(; //LEAN:Batteries.Util.LibraryNote ;)
Batteries__Util__LibraryNote : Set.

(; doc: The coercion from `F` to a function. ;)
(; //LEAN:DFunLike.coe ;)
DFunLike__coe : (x2 : Elem F) -> (a : Elem x) -> Elem (x1 a).

(; doc: The coercion to functions must be injective. ;)
(; //LEAN:DFunLike.coe_injective ;)
DFunLike__coe_injective : (F : Set) -> (x : Elem (outParam Set)) -> (x1 : (outParam (Elem x -> Set))) -> Proof (Function__Injective DFunLike__coe).

(; //LEAN:DFunLike.ext ;)
DFunLike__ext : (F : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Set)) -> (f : Elem F) -> (g : Elem F) -> (h : Proof (forall X (x1 => Eqt (x x1) (f x1) (g x1)))) -> Proof (Eqt F f g).

(; //LEAN:DFunLike.ext' ;)
DFunLike__ext' : (F : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Set)) -> (f : Elem F) -> (g : Elem F) -> (h : Proof (Eqt ((a : Elem X) -> x a) f g)) -> Proof (Eqt F f g).

(; doc: A `DivInvMonoid` is a `Monoid` with operations `/` and `⁻¹` satisfying `div_eq_mul_inv : ∀ a b, a / b = a * b⁻¹`.  This deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`. The default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b⁻¹` holds by definition.  Adding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b⁻¹` allows us to avoid certain classes of unification failures, for example: Let `Foo X` be a type with a `∀ X, Div (Foo X)` instance but no `∀ X, Inv (Foo X)`, e.g. when `Foo X` is a `EuclideanDomain`. Suppose we also have an instance `∀ X [Cromulent X], GroupWithZero (Foo X)`. Then the `(/)` coming from `GroupWithZero.div` cannot be definitionally equal to the `(/)` coming from `Foo.Div`.  In the same way, adding a `zpow` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures in diamonds. See the definition of `Monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more explanations on this. ;)
(; //LEAN:DivInvMonoid ;)
DivInvMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `DivisionMonoid` is a `DivInvMonoid` with involutive inversion and such that `(a * b)⁻¹ = b⁻¹ * a⁻¹` and `a * b = 1 → a⁻¹ = b`.  This is the immediate common ancestor of `Group` and `GroupWithZero`. ;)
(; //LEAN:DivisionMonoid ;)
DivisionMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: The equality relation. It has one introduction rule, `Eq.refl`. We use `a = b` as notation for `Eq a b`. A fundamental property of equality is that it is an equivalence relation. ``` variable (α : Type) (a b c d : α) variable (hab : a = b) (hcb : c = b) (hcd : c = d)  example : a = d :=   Eq.trans (Eq.trans hab (Eq.symm hcb)) hcd ``` Equality is much more than an equivalence relation, however. It has the important property that every assertion respects the equivalence, in the sense that we can substitute equal expressions without changing the truth value. That is, given `h1 : a = b` and `h2 : p a`, we can construct a proof for `p b` using substitution: `Eq.subst h1 h2`. Example: ``` example (α : Type) (a b : α) (p : α → Prop)         (h1 : a = b) (h2 : p a) : p b :=   Eq.subst h1 h2  example (α : Type) (a b : α) (p : α → Prop)     (h1 : a = b) (h2 : p a) : p b :=   h1 ▸ h2 ``` The triangle in the second presentation is a macro built on top of `Eq.subst` and `Eq.symm`, and you can enter it by typing `\t`. For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality)  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `=` in identifiers is `eq`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq ;)
Eq : (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mp : α → β` is the induced "cast" operation, mapping elements of `α` to elements of `β`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mp` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mp ;)
Eq__mp : (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mpr : β → α` is the induced "cast" operation in the reverse direction, mapping elements of `β` to elements of `α`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mpr` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mpr ;)
Eq__mpr : (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> Elem X.

(; doc: Equality is transitive: if `a = b` and `b = c` then `a = c`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have variables or expressions `h₁ : a = b` and `h₂ : b = c`, you can use `h₁.trans h₂ : a = c` as shorthand for `Eq.trans h₁ h₂`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.trans ;)
Eq__trans : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X b c)) -> Proof (Eqt X a c).

(; doc: The class `FunLike F α β` (`Fun`ction-`Like`) expresses that terms of type `F` have an injective coercion to functions from `α` to `β`. `FunLike` is the non-dependent version of `DFunLike`. This typeclass is used in the definition of the homomorphism typeclasses, such as `ZeroHomClass`, `MulHomClass`, `MonoidHomClass`, .... ;)
(; //LEAN:FunLike ;)
FunLike : (F : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> Set.

(; //LEAN:Function.Commute.iterate_left ;)
Function__Commute__iterate_left : (X : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> X)) -> (g : (Elem X -> X)) -> (h : Proof (Function__Commute f g)) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Function__Commute (Nat__iterate f n) g).

(; doc: A function `f : α → β` is called injective if `f x = f y` implies `x = y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Injective ;)
Function__Injective : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Function.Injective.eq_iff' ;)
Function__Injective__eq_iff' : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (I : Proof (Function__Injective f)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem Y) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Y (f b) c)) -> Proof (iff (Eqt Y (f a) c) (Eqt X a b)).

(; //LEAN:Function.Injective.of_comp ;)
Function__Injective__of_comp : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem Z -> X)) -> (I : Proof (Function__Injective (Function__comp f g))) -> Proof (Function__Injective g).

(; doc: `LeftInverse g f` means that `g` is a left inverse to `f`. That is, `g ∘ f = id`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.LeftInverse ;)
Function__LeftInverse : (g : (Elem Y -> X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `RightInverse g f` means that `g` is a right inverse to `f`. That is, `f ∘ g = id`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.RightInverse ;)
Function__RightInverse : (g : (Elem Y -> X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Function.Semiconj₂.iterate ;)
Function__Semiconj_u2082___iterate : (X : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> X)) -> (op : (Elem X -> Elem X -> X)) -> (hf : Proof (Function__Semiconj_u2082_ f op op)) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Function__Semiconj_u2082_ (Nat__iterate f n) op op).

(; doc: A function `f : α → β` is called surjective if every `b : β` is equal to `f a` for some `a : α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Surjective ;)
Function__Surjective : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: Function composition, usually written with the infix operator `∘`. A new function is created from two existing functions, where one function's output is used as input to the other.  Examples:  * `Function.comp List.reverse (List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  * `(List.reverse ∘ List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∘` in identifiers is `comp`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.comp ;)
Function__comp : (f : (Elem Y -> W)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem W.

(; //LEAN:Function.iterate_fixed ;)
Function__iterate_fixed : (X : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> X)) -> (x : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X (f x) x)) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Eqt X (Nat__iterate f n x) x).

(; doc: A `Group` is a `Monoid` with an operation `⁻¹` satisfying `a⁻¹ * a = 1`.  There is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b⁻¹`, with a default so that `a / b = a * b⁻¹` holds by definition.  Use `Group.ofLeftAxioms` or `Group.ofRightAxioms` to define a group structure on a type with the minimum proof obligations.  [Wikidata Q83478](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q83478) ;)
(; //LEAN:Group ;)
Group : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `a • b` computes the product of `a` and `b`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent, but it is intended to be used for left actions.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `•` in identifiers is `smul`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HSMul.hSMul ;)
HSMul__hSMul : (x1 : Elem X) -> (x2 : Elem Y) -> Elem x.

(; doc: An additive monoid has unique divisibility if scalar multiplication by every non-zero element `n : ℕ` is injective. This is the uniqueness counterpart to `DivisibleBy` which asserts existence.  `HasUniqueDiv` is stronger than `IsAddTorsionFree` for general additive monoids, but equivalent to it for commutative ones. Prefer using `HasUniqueDiv` for commutative additive monoids, so that typeclass-search can derive both `HasUniqueDiv` and `IsAddTorsionFree` without us needing to introduce an expensive `[AddCommMonoid M] [IsAddTorsionFree M] : HasUniqueDiv M` instance loop.  TODO: Generalize this definition to additive semigroups once we have the `PNat` action. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasUniqueDiv ;)
HasUniqueDiv : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A monoid has unique roots if exponentiation by every non-zero element `n : ℕ` is injective. This is the uniqueness counterpart to `RootableBy` which asserts existence.  `HasUniqueRoots` is stronger than `IsMulTorsionFree` for general monoids, but equivalent to it for commutative ones. Prefer using `HasUniqueRoots` for commutative monoids, so that typeclass-search can derive both `HasUniqueRoots` and `IsMulTorsionFree` without us needing to introduce an expensive `[CommMonoid M] [IsMulTorsionFree M] : HasUniqueRoots M` instance loop.  TODO: Generalize this definition to semigroups once we have the `PNat` action. ;)
(; //LEAN:HasUniqueRoots ;)
HasUniqueRoots : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:HasUniqueRoots.mk ;)
HasUniqueRoots__mk : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid M)) -> (pow_left_injective : Proof (forall Nat (n => if (Neq n 0) (Function__Injective (a => pow a n))))) -> Proof (HasUniqueRoots M).

(; //LEAN:HasUniqueRoots.pow_left_injective ;)
HasUniqueRoots__pow_left_injective : (M : Set) -> (x : Proof (Monoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (HasUniqueRoots M)) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> (hn : Proof (Neq n 0)) -> Proof (Function__Injective (a => pow a n)).

(; doc: **Alias** of `not_congr`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Iff.not ;)
Iff__not : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (iff (not a) (not b)).

(; doc: `default` is a function that produces a "default" element of any `Inhabited` type. This element does not have any particular specified properties, but it is often an all-zeroes value. ;)
(; //LEAN:Inhabited.default ;)
Inhabited__default : Elem X.

(; doc: `a⁻¹` computes the inverse of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `⁻¹` in identifiers is `inv`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Inv.inv ;)
Inv__inv : (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A Prop stating that the addition is commutative. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsAddCommutative ;)
IsAddCommutative : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An additive monoid is torsion-free if scalar multiplication by every non-zero element `n : ℕ` is injective on commuting elements (i.e., `a + b = b + a → n • a = n • b → a = b`).  For commutative additive monoids, this is equivalent to `n • a = n • b → a = b`. For additive groups, this is equivalent to `n • a = 0 → a = 0`.  Thus, this definition reconciles the notions of torsion-free for additive groups and commutative additive semigroups.  For more information, see this mathoverflow answer: https://mathoverflow.net/a/377268/95685  `IsAddTorsionFree` is weaker than `HasUniqueDiv` for general additive monoids, but equivalent to it for commutative ones. Prefer using `HasUniqueDiv` for commutative additive monoids, so that typeclass-search can derive both `HasUniqueDiv` and `IsAddTorsionFree` without us needing to introduce an expensive `[AddCommMonoid M] [IsAddTorsionFree M] : HasUniqueDiv M` instance loop.  TODO: Generalize this definition to additive semigroups once we have the `PNat` action. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsAddTorsionFree ;)
IsAddTorsionFree : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An additive monoid is Dedekind-finite if every left inverse is also a right inverse. Also called von Neumann-finite or directly finite. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsDedekindFiniteAddMonoid ;)
IsDedekindFiniteAddMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A monoid is Dedekind-finite if every left inverse is also a right inverse. It is more common to talk about Dedekind-finite rings, but https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.01598 does define Dedekind-finite monoids in §2.2. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsDedekindFiniteMonoid ;)
IsDedekindFiniteMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A Prop stating that the multiplication is commutative. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsMulCommutative ;)
IsMulCommutative : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A monoid is torsion-free if exponentiation by every non-zero element `n : ℕ` is injective on commuting elements (i.e., `a * b = b * a → a ^ n = b ^ n → a = b`).  For commutative monoids, this is equivalent to `a ^ n = b ^ n → a = b`. For groups, this is equivalent to `a ^ n = 1 → a = 1`.  Thus, this definition reconciles the notions of torsion-free for groups and commutative semigroups.  For more information, see this mathoverflow answer: https://mathoverflow.net/a/377268/95685  `IsMulTorsionFree` is weaker than `HasUniqueRoots` for general monoids, but equivalent to it for commutative ones. Prefer using `HasUniqueRoots` over commutative monoids, so that typeclass-search can derive both `HasUniqueRoots` and `IsMulTorsionFree` without us needing to introduce an expensive `[CommMonoid M] [IsMulTorsionFree M] : HasUniqueRoots M` instance loop.  TODO: Generalize this definition to semigroups once we have the `PNat` action. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsMulTorsionFree ;)
IsMulTorsionFree : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Although `TrailingParserDescr` is an abbreviation for `ParserDescr`, Lean will look at the declared type in order to determine whether to add the parser to the leading or trailing parser table. The determination is done automatically by the `syntax` command. ;)
(; //LEAN:Lean.TrailingParserDescr ;)
Lean__TrailingParserDescr : Set.

(; doc: A `Monoid` is a `Semigroup` with an element `1` such that `1 * a = a * 1 = a`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Monoid ;)
Monoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:MonoidHom.comp._proof_1 ;)
MonoidHom__comp___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (P : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne M)) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne N)) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne P)) -> (hnp : Elem (MonoidHom N P)) -> (hmn : Elem (MonoidHom M N)) -> Proof (Eqt P (hnp (hmn 1)) 1).

(; //LEAN:MonoidHom.comp._proof_2 ;)
MonoidHom__comp___proof_2 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (P : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne M)) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne N)) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne P)) -> (hnp : Elem (MonoidHom N P)) -> (hmn : Elem (MonoidHom M N)) -> (x3 : Elem M) -> (y : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt P (hnp (hmn (times x3 y))) (times (hnp (hmn x3)) (hnp (hmn y)))).

(; //LEAN:MonoidHom.copy._proof_1 ;)
MonoidHom__copy___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne M)) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne N)) -> (f : Elem (MonoidHom M N)) -> (f' : (Elem M -> N)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Elem M -> N) f' f)) -> (x2 : Elem M) -> (y : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt N (MulHom__toFun (MulHom__copy (MonoidHom__toMulHom f) f' h) (times x2 y)) (times (MulHom__toFun (MulHom__copy (MonoidHom__toMulHom f) f' h) x2) (MulHom__toFun (MulHom__copy (MonoidHom__toMulHom f) f' h) y))).

(; //LEAN:MonoidHom.id._proof_1 ;)
MonoidHom__id___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne M)) -> Proof (Eqt M 1 1).

(; //LEAN:MonoidHom.id._proof_2 ;)
MonoidHom__id___proof_2 : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne M)) -> (x1 : Elem M) -> (x2 : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (times x1 x2) (times x1 x2)).

(; //LEAN:MonoidHom.inverse._proof_3 ;)
MonoidHom__inverse___proof_3 : (A : Set) -> (B : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid A)) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid B)) -> (f : Elem (MonoidHom A B)) -> (g : (Elem B -> A)) -> (h : Proof (Function__LeftInverse g f)) -> Proof (Eqt A (OneHom__toFun (OneHom__inverse (OneHomClass__toOneHom f) g h) 1) 1).

(; //LEAN:MonoidHom.inverse._proof_4 ;)
MonoidHom__inverse___proof_4 : (A : Set) -> (B : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid A)) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid B)) -> (f : Elem (MonoidHom A B)) -> (g : (Elem B -> A)) -> (h : Proof (Function__LeftInverse g f)) -> (h1 : Proof (Function__RightInverse g f)) -> (x2 : Elem B) -> (y : Elem B) -> Proof (Eqt A (MulHom__toFun (MulHom__inverse (MulHomClass__toMulHom f) g h h1) (times x2 y)) (times (MulHom__toFun (MulHom__inverse (MulHomClass__toMulHom f) g h h1) x2) (MulHom__toFun (MulHom__inverse (MulHomClass__toMulHom f) g h h1) y))).

(; //LEAN:MonoidHom.mk'._proof_1 ;)
MonoidHom__mk'___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (_ : Proof (MulOneClass M)) -> (f : (Elem M -> G)) -> (map_mul : Proof (forall M (a => forall M (b => Eqt G (f (times a b)) (times (f a) (f b)))))) -> Proof (Eqt G (f 1) 1).

(; //LEAN:MonoidHom.ofClass._proof_1 ;)
MonoidHom__ofClass___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne M)) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne N)) -> (f : Elem F) -> Proof (Eqt N (OneHom__toFun (OneHomClass__toOneHom f) 1) 1).

(; //LEAN:MonoidHom.ofClass._proof_2 ;)
MonoidHom__ofClass___proof_2 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (F : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne M)) -> (_ : Proof (MulOne N)) -> (f : Elem F) -> (x3 : Elem M) -> (y : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt N (MulHom__toFun (MulHomClass__toMulHom f) (times x3 y)) (times (MulHom__toFun (MulHomClass__toMulHom f) x3) (MulHom__toFun (MulHomClass__toMulHom f) y))).

(; doc: The homogeneous version of `HMul`: `a * b : α` where `a b : α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Mul ;)
Mul : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:MulHom.comp._proof_1 ;)
MulHom__comp___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (P : Set) -> (hnp : Elem (MulHom N P)) -> (hmn : Elem (MulHom M N)) -> (x3 : Elem M) -> (y : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt P (hnp (hmn (times x3 y))) (times (hnp (hmn x3)) (hnp (hmn y)))).

(; //LEAN:MulHom.copy._proof_1 ;)
MulHom__copy___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (f : Elem (MulHom M N)) -> (f' : (Elem M -> N)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Elem M -> N) f' f)) -> (x2 : Elem M) -> (y : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt N (f' (times x2 y)) (times (f' x2) (f' y))).

(; //LEAN:MulHom.id._proof_1 ;)
MulHom__id___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (x1 : Elem M) -> (x2 : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (times x1 x2) (times x1 x2)).

(; //LEAN:MulHom.inverse._proof_3 ;)
MulHom__inverse___proof_3 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (f : Elem (MulHom M N)) -> (g : (Elem N -> M)) -> (h : Proof (Function__LeftInverse g f)) -> (h1 : Proof (Function__RightInverse g f)) -> (x2 : Elem N) -> (y : Elem N) -> Proof (Eqt M (g (times x2 y)) (times (g x2) (g y))).

(; doc: Bundling a `Mul` and `One` structure together without any axioms about their compatibility. See `MulOneClass` for the additional assumption that 1 is an identity. ;)
(; //LEAN:MulOne ;)
MulOne : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Typeclass for expressing that a type `M` with multiplication and a one satisfies `1 * a = a` and `a * 1 = a` for all `a : M`. ;)
(; //LEAN:MulOneClass ;)
MulOneClass : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Iterate a function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.iterate ;)
Nat__iterate : (op : (Elem X -> X)) -> (x : Elem Nat) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `-a` computes the negative or opposite of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `-` in identifiers is `neg` (when used as a unary operator). ;)
(; //LEAN:Neg.neg ;)
Neg__neg : (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A type with a "one" element. ;)
(; //LEAN:One ;)
One : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:OneHom.comp._proof_1 ;)
OneHom__comp___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (P : Set) -> (hnp : Elem (OneHom N P)) -> (hmn : Elem (OneHom M N)) -> Proof (Eqt P (hnp (hmn 1)) 1).

(; //LEAN:OneHom.copy._proof_1 ;)
OneHom__copy___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (f : Elem (OneHom M N)) -> (f' : (Elem M -> N)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Elem M -> N) f' f)) -> Proof (Eqt N (f' 1) 1).

(; //LEAN:OneHom.id._proof_1 ;)
OneHom__id___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> Proof (Eqt M 1 1).

(; //LEAN:OneHom.inverse._proof_1 ;)
OneHom__inverse___proof_1 : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (f : Elem (OneHom M N)) -> (g : (Elem N -> M)) -> (h : Proof (Function__LeftInverse g f)) -> Proof (Eqt M (g 1) 1).

(; doc: A `SubNegMonoid` is an `AddMonoid` with unary `-` and binary `-` operations satisfying `sub_eq_add_neg : ∀ a b, a - b = a + -b`.  The default for `sub` is such that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.  Adding `sub` as a field rather than defining `a - b := a + -b` allows us to avoid certain classes of unification failures, for example: Let `foo X` be a type with a `∀ X, Sub (Foo X)` instance but no `∀ X, Neg (Foo X)`. Suppose we also have an instance `∀ X [Cromulent X], AddGroup (Foo X)`. Then the `(-)` coming from `AddGroup.sub` cannot be definitionally equal to the `(-)` coming from `Foo.Sub`.  In the same way, adding a `zsmul` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures in diamonds. See the definition of `AddMonoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more explanations on this. ;)
(; //LEAN:SubNegMonoid ;)
SubNegMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A _subsingleton_ is a type with at most one element. It is either empty or has a unique element.  All propositions are subsingletons because of proof irrelevance: false propositions are empty, and all proofs of a true proposition are equal to one another. Some non-propositional types are also subsingletons. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subsingleton ;)
Subsingleton : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: If a type is a subsingleton, then all of its elements are equal. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subsingleton.elim ;)
Subsingleton__elim : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Subsingleton X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a b).

(; doc: Prove that `α` is a subsingleton by showing that any two elements are equal. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subsingleton.intro ;)
Subsingleton__intro : (X : Set) -> (allEq : Proof (forall X (a => forall X (b => Eqt X a b)))) -> Proof (Subsingleton X).

(; doc: A `SubtractionMonoid` is a `SubNegMonoid` with involutive negation and such that `-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 → -a = b`. ;)
(; //LEAN:SubtractionMonoid ;)
SubtractionMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `True` is a proposition and has only an introduction rule, `True.intro : True`. In other words, `True` is simply true, and has a canonical proof, `True.intro` For more information: [Propositional Logic](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/propositions_and_proofs.html#propositional-logic) ;)
(; //LEAN:True ;)
True : Prop.

(; doc: A type with a zero element. ;)
(; //LEAN:Zero ;)
Zero : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Congruence in both function and argument. If `f₁ = f₂` and `a₁ = a₂` then `f₁ a₁ = f₂ a₂`. This only works for nondependent functions; the theorem statement is more complex in the dependent case.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congr ;)
congr : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x1 : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> (x3 : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Elem X -> Y) x x1)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X x2 x3)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (x x2) (x1 x3)).

(; doc: Congruence in the function argument: if `a₁ = a₂` then `f a₁ = f a₂` for any (nondependent) function `f`. This is more powerful than it might look at first, because you can also use a lambda expression for `f` to prove that `<something containing a₁> = <something containing a₂>`. This function is used internally by tactics like `congr` and `simp` to apply equalities inside subterms.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congrArg ;)
congrArg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; //LEAN:eq_inv_of_mul_eq_one_left ;)
eq_inv_of_mul_eq_one_left : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (DivisionMonoid G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (h : Proof (Eqt G (times a b) 1)) -> Proof (Eqt G a (Inv__inv b)).

(; //LEAN:eq_self ;)
eq_self : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (Eqt X a a) True).

(; doc: The identity function. `id` takes an implicit argument `α : Sort u` (a type in any universe), and an argument `a : α`, and returns `a`.  Although this may look like a useless function, one application of the identity function is to explicitly put a type on an expression. If `e` has type `T`, and `T'` is definitionally equal to `T`, then `@id T' e` typechecks, and Lean knows that this expression has type `T'` rather than `T`. This can make a difference for typeclass inference, since `T` and `T'` may have different typeclass instances on them. `show T' from e` is sugar for an `@id T' e` expression. ;)
(; //LEAN:id ;)
id : (a : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:inv_mul_cancel ;)
inv_mul_cancel : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (times (Inv__inv a) a) 1).

(; //LEAN:ne_of_apply_ne ;)
ne_of_apply_ne : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x : Elem X) -> (y : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Neq (f x) (f y))) -> Proof (Neq x y).

(; //LEAN:of_eq_true ;)
of_eq_true : (p : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Prop p True)) -> Proof p.

(; doc: Gadget for marking output parameters in type classes.  For example, the `Membership` class is defined as: ``` class Membership (α : outParam (Type u)) (γ : Type v) ``` This means that whenever a typeclass goal of the form `Membership ?α ?γ` comes up, Lean will wait to solve it until `?γ` is known, but then it will run typeclass inference, and take the first solution it finds, for any value of `?α`, which thereby determines what `?α` should be.  This expresses that in a term like `a ∈ s`, `s` might be a `Set α` or `List α` or some other type with a membership operation, and in each case the "member" type `α` is determined by looking at the container type. ;)
(; //LEAN:outParam ;)
outParam : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: `rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the same as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.  This is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although the statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is definitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in Lean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality. ;)
(; //LEAN:rfl ;)
rfl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

